From: cormierjohn <49281283+cormierjohn@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:52:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] perf(compensation): compensate bottom layers on RLE runs,
 row-parallel

Bottom compensation decoded each layer's lit box to pixels, ran an exact
distance transform and a flood fill on one thread, and held a global lock so
only one layer was compensated at a time. A full-plate Saturn 4 Ultra 16K layer
took 2 to 3.2 s and about 1.7 GB.

It now works on each row's spans: solid spans are joined into regions with a
union-find, a walk over the regions gives each its nesting depth, and "within
d mm" is the spans widened by a circle in mm (an ellipse in non-square pixels),
row by row with rayon. The lock and the frame-sized buffers are gone; the same
layer takes 4 to 5 ms.

The f32 distance transform lost precision far from the frame origin and missed
a few pixels inside the distance. Of 238 pixels that differ from the old output
on that layer, all match the exact rule. A new test compares against a
pixel-by-pixel reference on random nested shapes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
